# rule.forbid.brackets-in-filenames

no filename may carry `[` or `]`. mark a document kind in coordinate form:

```
👍  kno601.inventories._.kind=article.md
👎  kno601.inventories._.[article].md
```

binds new and renamed files. an extant bracketed name in a dependency's briefs stays until disturbed; re-seed it to its owner.

## .why

brackets break glob, `boot.yml` entries, and shell; `mvsafe`/`cpsafe`/`rmsafe` need `--literal`. `kind=` is computable and globbable: `*.kind=article.md` returns the archetype.

| when… | then… |
|---|---|
| you name a new document with an archetype | `kind=$archetype` |
| you rename a bracketed file you own | take the coordinate form |
| you copy a name shape from a dependency | that tree's convention is not this one's |
| you need `--literal` or `\[` | the name is the defect |
| a glob returns fewer files than expected | check for brackets first |

## .drop the marker when the lead segment carries it

```
👍  philosophy.entoolment-is-the-pinnacle._.md   # not …kind=philosophy.md
👍  howto.curate-an-inventory.md                 # not …kind=guide.md
```

repair for `howto.$topic.[guide].md` is to drop the marker, not convert it. the `._.` cluster root stays (a different axis).

repair is file-first and by hand; a bulk rename cannot part a citation of a bad name from an instance.

governs filenames only. brackets in prose, links, tables, code blocks are untouched.

blocker: new or renamed filename with `[` or `]` · bracketed name copied from a dependency.
not a violation: extant bracketed name in a dependency's briefs · bracket in prose, link, or code.

⇒ `rule.forbid.itemization-without-coordinates`
